ago to go all Kubernetes.

00:09:35.932 --> 00:09:37.640
Can go into a bunch
of different reasons,

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.442
both technical and
business reasons.

00:09:40.442 --> 00:09:41.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, give us one.

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:42.230
QUINN SLACK: All right.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:43.650
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, please do.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.719
QUINN SLACK: So I am the CEO
and founder, but I'm also a dev

00:09:46.719 --> 00:09:48.260
and I've been coding
for a long time.

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:52.100
So I get to see both reasons
why we made this choice.

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.620
First, let's start out
with the technical reasons

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:56.060
for making the choice.

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:58.130
There's the developer
aesthetic reason.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:09:59.990
You look at
Kubernetes, and you can

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:03.700
tell that it's a deployment
system just built

00:10:03.700 --> 00:10:06.870
by people who've been thinking
about this problem for a decade

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:07.980
or more.

00:10:07.980 --> 00:10:10.200
And Google understands
how to build a solution

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.450
to this problem better than
probably any other company

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:13.680
out there.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:16.680
All these little things that
we never would have thought

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.327
of in our own
deployment system--

00:10:18.327 --> 00:10:20.160
and other systems
wouldn't have thought of--

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:21.417
Kubernetes has addressed.

00:10:21.417 --> 00:10:23.250
Or you can look at the
roadmap, and you know

00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:25.590
they're going to address it.

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:28.080
Love that it's written in Go,
that you can read the code,

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:30.290
that the CLI is really good.

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:33.090
I just feel like it was
built for people like us.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.330
And the other technical
reason is just--

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:37.830
it works very well.

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:39.940
It was easy to get
up and running.

00:10:39.940 --> 00:10:41.970
It is well-documented.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:43.560
It's a standard tool.

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.800
And then, of course,
we tried it out locally

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:49.440
and we wanted to see where
we could deploy this,

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:52.440
and we saw that Google
Cloud-- obviously, they

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:54.060
have native support
for Kubernetes,

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:56.310
and you get the feeling
that Google Cloud was

00:10:56.310 --> 00:10:58.120
built with Kubernetes in mind.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:11:00.791
So that's why we went with
it for technical reasons.

00:11:00.791 --> 00:11:01.290
Cool.

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.630
So you sound like
you're doing a bit more

00:11:03.630 --> 00:11:06.790
than, say, your standard web
app or something like that.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:08.310
How are you using Kubernetes?

00:11:08.310 --> 00:11:10.302
Are you using it
in stateless ways?

00:11:10.302 --> 00:11:11.760
Are you using it
for stateful ways?

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:12.801
Are you using deployment?

00:11:12.801 --> 00:11:13.440
StatefulSets?

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.260
How-- are you
using the Jobs API?

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:17.970
How are you using GKE?

00:11:17.970 --> 00:11:21.120
We started using it two years
ago when a lot of these things

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:25.290
were not available, and we
have a few design decisions

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.900
we made with our application
that make things easier for us.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:30.570
We actually don't need
to store a lot of state.

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:33.900
So when you go on Sourcegraph
and you mouseover something

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:35.460
and you see a hover,
that's actually

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:39.120
being served in real time
from a language server

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:41.910
process running somewhere
in our Kubernetes cluster.

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:43.500
So not a lot of state.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.320
And the tiny amount of data
that we do need to store,

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:51.660
it's either one index around
cross-browser references,

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:53.190
or just a cache.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:57.000
So we have our web server
frontend process running.

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:00.420
That has GraphQL
and WebSocket APIs.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.360
The WebSocket one is for the
language server protocol.

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:07.590
We have a proxy that the web
server will send a language

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:10.290
server request to, and then
the proxy will figure out,

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.330
do I need to spin up
another Go process

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.730
or Go Server to serve that?

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.970
And then it'll send it to one
of the language server backends,

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:21.480
and that will fetch all the
repo data from our GET server,

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:24.150
also running in the same
cluster in Kubernetes.

00:12:24.150 --> 00:12:26.250
And it'll do all the
language analysis,

00:12:26.250 --> 00:12:30.120
whether that's in Go or Java
or TypeScript or Python or C#

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:34.620
or whatever, and it'll return
back through the web socket

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:35.840
and back to the client.

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:36.756
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:12:36.756 --> 00:12:40.320
So I'm wondering-- you mentioned
that you like Kubernetes partly

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.491
because [? it's read and ?]
[? go. ?] Is Sourcegraph also

00:12:43.491 --> 00:12:44.490
[? read and ?] [? go? ?]

00:12:44.490 --> 00:12:45.420
QUINN SLACK: Oh, yeah.

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.210
From day one, we wrote
Sourcegraph in Go.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:51.500
And initially, we chose
Go because Sourcegraph

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.250
is something that
understands source code,

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:55.361
and Go has amazing libraries--

00:12:55.361 --> 00:12:57.360
even in the standard
library-- for understanding

00:12:57.360 --> 00:12:58.530
its own source code.

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:00.390
So it was the natural choice.

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:03.109
And we also had been coming--

00:13:03.109 --> 00:13:04.650
my co-founder and
I-- from jobs where

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:07.530
we were writing other languages,
which will not be named.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:11.070
And Go is really nice
to start working in.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:14.520
And since then, we've
grown to other languages.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:15.340
We still love Go.

00:13:15.340 --> 00:13:17.355
We still write all
of our stuff in Go,

00:13:17.355 --> 00:13:19.440
but we have customers
and users across a bunch

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:20.648
of different other languages.

00:13:20.648 --> 00:13:24.570
And generally, when we write
the Python language server,

00:13:24.570 --> 00:13:25.965
we'll do that in Python.

00:13:25.965 --> 00:13:26.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:13:26.370 --> 00:13:28.869
QUINN SLACK: So we are polyglot,
but we have a special place

00:13:28.869 --> 00:13:29.890
in our heart for Go.

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:31.050
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
I guess that's also

00:13:31.050 --> 00:13:33.080
one of the benefits of
running in Kubernetes,

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:34.920
is no matter what
language you have,

00:13:34.920 --> 00:13:37.440
you put it into a Go container
and then you're ready to go.

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:37.650
QUINN SLACK: Yeah.

00:13:37.650 --> 00:13:38.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:13:38.460 --> 00:13:39.780
QUINN SLACK: Yeah, so I want
to talk about the business

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.520
reasons, too, for Kubernetes.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:42.120
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, go for it!

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:42.828
QUINN SLACK: Yep.

00:13:42.828 --> 00:13:44.960
As you know, I got
to think about both.

00:13:44.960 --> 00:13:47.820
And our product
is obviously loved

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:49.300
by a lot of people
in open source,

00:13:49.300 --> 00:13:50.580
but it is for companies.

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:52.540
It is for companies
with a lot of code.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.650
And for those companies,
it is important to them

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:57.030
that their code stays secure.

00:13:57.030 --> 00:14:00.270
It's important that they run
a lot of their core services

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:02.100
in their own infrastructure.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:05.790
And it is a really important
business decision for us--

00:14:05.790 --> 00:14:08.590
how we deliver our software
to these customers.

00:14:08.590 --> 00:14:10.950
We wanted to make
sure that we built it

00:14:10.950 --> 00:14:12.810
so that it is so
easy for a customer

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:14.070
to run Sourcegraph internally.

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:17.820
And that meant not being
tied to any specific vendor's

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:21.780
way of deploying or
orchestrating an application.

00:14:21.780 --> 00:14:26.160
So if we have customers across
all different kinds of Clouds,

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:30.490
then by using Kubernetes,
that is the standard.

00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:33.300
And it's not the case that every
single one of our customers

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:37.080
is already using Kubernetes
and we can just slot that in.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:40.020
But it is a very easy
sell to them to say,

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:42.810
we are built on Kubernetes
and we'll ship this to you

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:44.310
as a Helm chart.

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:47.490
And luckily our
audience is developers,

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:48.526
and so they get that.

00:14:48.526 --> 00:14:50.400
And even if they're not
using Kubernetes now,

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:53.477
they can sense that they will
be using it in the future.

00:14:53.477 --> 00:14:55.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, you
just stole my question.

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:58.230
I was going to ask you--
because you were saying, well,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:01.350
one of the reasons is-- we want
to run it in Kubernetes so it's

00:15:01.350 --> 00:15:03.310
easy to install it
and run it anywhere

00:15:03.310 --> 00:15:04.670
where they have Kubernetes.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:07.700
And you mentioned
Helm, which I love.

00:15:07.700 --> 00:15:11.540
We had an episode on
Helm some time ago.

00:15:11.540 --> 00:15:14.150
I'm sure you can find
it if you search for it.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:15.157
Why are you using Helm?

00:15:15.157 --> 00:15:17.490
Tell us a little bit more
about the experience using it.

00:15:17.490 --> 00:15:18.680
I'm curious.

00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:20.990
QUINN SLACK: Well, I don't
know as much about Helm.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:22.190
I am a user of it.

00:15:22.190 --> 00:15:24.706
I was not the person who
created our Helm charts.

00:15:24.706 --> 00:15:26.330
That was one of our
teammates, Richard,

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:29.270
who also works on GopherJS--

00:15:29.270 --> 00:15:32.030
the Go JavaScript compiler.

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:33.170
MARK MANDEL: Oh, cool.

00:15:33.170 --> 00:15:33.920
QUINN SLACK: Yeah.

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:35.890
You should direct any
questions on that to him.

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:36.890
He's the expert on that.

00:15:36.890 --> 00:15:38.098
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Really cool.

00:15:38.098 --> 00:15:40.460
So for those that are
interested in Helm,

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:43.659
we actually had an
episode-- according to Mark.

00:15:43.659 --> 00:15:44.325
Thank you, Mark.

00:15:44.325 --> 00:15:45.770
MARK MANDEL: That's all right.

00:15:45.770 --> 00:15:47.210
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Episode 50.

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:49.430
We have Michelle Noorali
and Matthew Butcher.

00:15:49.430 --> 00:15:52.220
So go check it out if
you're curious about Helm.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:55.987
I say that Helm is basically
APT-GET for Kubernetes.

00:15:55.987 --> 00:15:57.570
I don't know if
that's correct or not,

00:15:57.570 --> 00:15:59.049
but I like to say
it's like that.

00:15:59.049 --> 00:16:00.215
MARK MANDEL: That's correct.

00:16:00.215 --> 00:16:00.715
Cool.

00:16:00.715 --> 00:16:03.950
So it sounds like you're using,
basically, open source, which

00:16:03.950 --> 00:16:06.299
is awesome, for running
your applications.

00:16:06.299 --> 00:16:07.715
What are you using
for monitoring?

00:16:07.715 --> 00:16:10.460
Are you using our stuff, or
do you use something else?

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:12.230
QUINN SLACK: We use
LightStep, which

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:17.000
is based on the open-source
protocol for tracing.

00:16:17.000 --> 00:16:19.100
And we've instrumented
our language servers,

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:22.670
our Go servers, and even
some of our infrastructure,

00:16:22.670 --> 00:16:23.930
to just send up traces.

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:26.990
And I know that that was built
by some former Google Cloud

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:29.930
people, and it's
based on a system

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:31.710
that you guys have at Google.

00:16:31.710 --> 00:16:33.740
So I'd like to think
that it's still somewhat

00:16:33.740 --> 00:16:35.844
in the Google Cloud family,
but we love LightStep.

00:16:35.844 --> 00:16:36.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:39.093
At some point, you mentioned
a little bit about the fact

00:16:39.093 --> 00:16:41.600
that you were
storing some cache.

00:16:41.600 --> 00:16:43.490
Where are you
storing-- in general?

00:16:43.490 --> 00:16:46.725
What kind of storage are
you using for Sourcegraph?

00:16:46.725 --> 00:16:48.350
QUINN SLACK: We have
a couple pods that

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.360
have Postgres servers in
our Kubernetes cluster,

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:53.220
and they do not use any
special kind of persistence.

00:16:53.220 --> 00:16:55.980
If those end up dying
for whatever reason,

00:16:55.980 --> 00:16:57.590
then they'll just
come right back up

00:16:57.590 --> 00:16:59.570
and we'll kick off
the indexing jobs.

00:16:59.570 --> 00:17:01.460
We built everything
about our infrastructure

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:05.390
so that if the data went away--
even if the global index went

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:08.690
away-- that we could
recreate it in minutes, even

00:17:08.690 --> 00:17:11.359
for all of the open-source
code that matters.

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:13.520
So system design was
really important.

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.950
If we hadn't
optimized the system

00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:20.370
to be super fast on the fly for
answering any specific question

00:17:20.370 --> 00:17:22.369
and to do only the minimal
amount of computation

00:17:22.369 --> 00:17:24.200
necessary to answer
that question,

00:17:24.200 --> 00:17:26.780
then I think we would have to
think more about persistence.

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:29.520
We'd have to at least
have our Kubernetes

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:31.440
setup be a lot more complex.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:32.930
It'd be a lot more fragile.

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:37.250
But because we built our system
around not persisting data,

00:17:37.250 --> 00:17:38.729
it makes our job a lot easier.

00:17:38.729 --> 00:17:39.770
MARK MANDEL: Interesting.

00:17:39.770 --> 00:17:43.610
And I know, as well, you said
you've got a Chrome plug-in.

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:45.920
I know you've also got
some IDE integrations.

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:48.606
How are you making
that connection,

00:17:48.606 --> 00:17:50.480
monitoring that connection,
checking latency?

00:17:50.480 --> 00:17:53.950
How is that whole pathway
from, I'm going to assume GKE,

00:17:53.950 --> 00:17:58.010
up into the browser and/or
your IDE integrations, as well?

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:00.260
QUINN SLACK: This is one of
those classic CS interview

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:01.310
questions.

00:18:01.310 --> 00:18:02.810
Tell me everything
that happens when

00:18:02.810 --> 00:18:05.661
you type in www.Google.com.

00:18:05.661 --> 00:18:07.760
So from the user's
point of view,

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:11.060
they have our Chrome extension,
and they go to GitHub.

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:13.890
They put their mouse over,
say, a function call.

00:18:13.890 --> 00:18:17.990
What happens there is that will
issue just a normal HTTP post

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:21.530
request to our servers.

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:25.640
A post request-- it's like a
single-shot LSP session using

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.870
the Language Server
Protocol to say,

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:29.510
hey, get me this
hover for this file,

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:31.260
and this repo at this character.

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.260
That will go to our
frontend server,

00:18:33.260 --> 00:18:37.010
and then that will open a web
socket via this language server

00:18:37.010 --> 00:18:39.690
proxy that we have to the
language server backend.

00:18:39.690 --> 00:18:42.950
It'll spin up a language server
for that repo with that commit,

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:43.910
fetch the files.

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:45.380
Again, fetching
the minimum set of

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:47.730
files necessary to
answer that question.

00:18:47.730 --> 00:18:50.480
And then it'll return
the data, and you'll just

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:52.970
get a single HTTP
response on the client.

00:18:52.970 --> 00:18:54.990
And then you'll show
that in a little tooltip.

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:56.280
MARK MANDEL: So now I'm
just actually curious--

00:18:56.280 --> 00:18:58.670
that WebSocket connection,
is that internal,

00:18:58.670 --> 00:19:00.357
or is that to the browser?

00:19:00.357 --> 00:19:01.940
QUINN SLACK: It
depends on the client.

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:05.060
Our Chrome extension doesn't
open a WebSocket to our server.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:08.840
And if you're on Sourcegraph.com
or our desktop app,

00:19:08.840 --> 00:19:11.330
then that will open a WebSocket.

00:19:11.330 --> 00:19:14.150
No good reason for doing
WebSocket versus not,

00:19:14.150 --> 00:19:17.141
other than we look at
average duration of session.

00:19:17.141 --> 00:19:18.890
And we can optimize
it a little bit better

00:19:18.890 --> 00:19:21.685
if it's just a single
HTTP post request,

00:19:21.685 --> 00:19:23.060
then we know that
we can clean up

00:19:23.060 --> 00:19:24.800
the resources when that's done.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:26.570
And generally,
the average number

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:28.880
of hovers that someone does
in our Chrome extension

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:31.010
is less than the
number of hovers

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:33.860
they do on an average
Sourcegraph.com session.

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:35.380
Just some internal
stuff like that.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:36.296
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:19:36.296 --> 00:19:38.840
So still talking about the
same topic of how you connect

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:42.030
from your clients
to your servers,

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.940
you said that
you're using mostly

00:19:43.940 --> 00:19:47.030
either HTTP requests
like one-off requests,

00:19:47.030 --> 00:19:48.860
or using WebSockets.

00:19:48.860 --> 00:19:51.340
I'm curious, are
you using HTTP/2?

00:19:51.340 --> 00:19:56.180
Why not HTTP/2 with streaming
or maybe even gRPC on top of it?

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:57.950
QUINN SLACK: Well,
we do use HTTP/2.

00:19:57.950 --> 00:20:01.100
We have the server do push
for some of our assets.

00:20:01.100 --> 00:20:03.870
And that results in a
pretty nice performance

00:20:03.870 --> 00:20:05.880
increase on the initial load.

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:09.870
We use WebSockets for
LSP, because there

00:20:09.870 --> 00:20:12.240
were good libraries
to do that already.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:15.180
We used to use gRPC
for some things like,

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.130
fetch me a list of
the repositories

00:20:17.130 --> 00:20:18.810
that a user can view.

00:20:18.810 --> 00:20:22.170
But so much of the
client activity

00:20:22.170 --> 00:20:24.690
was occurring from
browser clients.

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:27.600
And at the time, gRPC
didn't have good support--

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:31.020
either for issuing
requests or for making

00:20:31.020 --> 00:20:34.010
a RESTful API, which we still
needed for other reasons.

00:20:34.010 --> 00:20:38.010
The gRPC HTTP gateway project
looked super exciting,

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:41.490
but by the time that we needed
some things out of our API

00:20:41.490 --> 00:20:44.180
that it didn't support, it
wasn't far enough along.

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:45.030
MARK MANDEL: That
seems reasonable.

00:20:45.030 --> 00:20:45.946
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:20:45.946 --> 00:20:48.349
So we've talked a little bit
about monitoring and tracing

00:20:48.349 --> 00:20:49.890
and all of these
things will generate

00:20:49.890 --> 00:20:51.430
a decent amount of data.

00:20:51.430 --> 00:20:53.760
So, I'm curious about what
do you do with that data

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.020
afterwards?

00:20:55.020 --> 00:20:57.270
QUINN SLACK: Well, for things
like performance tracing

00:20:57.270 --> 00:21:01.800
and event logging, we use
other services like LightStep

00:21:01.800 --> 00:21:06.180
that just collect all the data,
and we offload that to them.

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:09.720
This is for Sourcegraph.com
so this is Cloud data.

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:13.200
It's just sent over
gRPC protocol using open

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:15.390
tracing to LightStep,
and then we

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:17.460
can see, actually,
for any operation that

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:21.450
occurs on the site what was the
entire trace of every operation

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:22.340
that happened.

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:24.150
There's actually
a debug setting,

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:26.430
if you are an intrepid
Sourcegraph user where

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:29.490
you can turn on the ability
to get little LightStep links

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:31.812
to any operation you perform.

00:21:31.812 --> 00:21:34.020
And there have been some
times when we've asked users

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:36.960
to turn that on and it's been
super useful to collect that.

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:39.210
MARK MANDEL: Do you store
it in any intermediary step?

00:21:39.210 --> 00:21:42.420
Do you use Google Cloud Storage,
or is it just sent directly off

00:21:42.420 --> 00:21:44.310
to your third-party services?

00:21:44.310 --> 00:21:46.410
QUINN SLACK: Yeah, right
now some of the logs

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:48.730
are stored in a bucket
on Google Cloud Storage,

00:21:48.730 --> 00:21:52.650
and then that gets
ingested into BigQuery.

00:21:52.650 --> 00:21:54.370
We have some other
intermediate jobs

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:58.140
to associate different
machines' log data,

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.530
apply other transformations
to it, normalizations.

00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:03.240
And then that gets
into BigQuery.

00:22:03.240 --> 00:22:07.320
And then on top of BigQuery, we
will run some automated queries

00:22:07.320 --> 00:22:10.800
on BigQuery to do reporting,
and that shows up in our Slack.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:14.340
And we'll also look at the data
through a bunch of dashboards

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:15.549
that we've created on Looker.

00:22:15.549 --> 00:22:16.464
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:22:16.464 --> 00:22:18.810
So does that mean that most
of the logging that you do

00:22:18.810 --> 00:22:20.260
is structured logging?

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:21.010
QUINN SLACK: Yeah.

00:22:21.010 --> 00:22:21.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:23.010
So you're logging
structurally, you

00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:26.940
send it to BigQuery, and just
keep it there for reports.

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:30.420
But do you ever use
it as a debug tool

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:33.106
to figure out what happened to
a request, or not that much?

00:22:33.106 --> 00:22:34.230
QUINN SLACK: Not that much.

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:36.521
Maybe everyone thinks that
they're special in this way,

00:22:36.521 --> 00:22:39.950
but because so much of
our system is real time,

00:22:39.950 --> 00:22:41.910
there's not a lot
of persistent data.

00:22:41.910 --> 00:22:44.370
For most things, we can
reproduce the issues

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:46.470
by just running things
locally because it

00:22:46.470 --> 00:22:49.440
doesn't depend on state
that's stored somewhere else.

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:51.510
And we've built our
system so that we

00:22:51.510 --> 00:22:53.470
have a lot of test
cases of, here

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:55.440
is a really complicated
Java project.

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:58.320
Can you produce the right
hover for this token?

00:22:58.320 --> 00:23:01.110
And we try to make everything
a pure function as much

00:23:01.110 --> 00:23:02.160
as possible.

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:05.490
So we were actually just
talking with someone

00:23:05.490 --> 00:23:08.070
else who runs ops for a
pretty big company elsewhere

00:23:08.070 --> 00:23:11.790
in San Francisco, and he was
asking how much of your day

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:15.610
is spent on ops fire drills
and p zeroes and all that.

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:18.300
And I showed him our ops log
where we record every p zero,

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:20.952
and there are not a lot of them.

00:23:20.952 --> 00:23:23.410
I think that's because of the
way that we built our system,

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:26.610
and because we built it on a
bunch of primitive components

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:27.630
that work really well.

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:29.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Because
it's written in Go.

00:23:29.070 --> 00:23:31.050
QUINN SLACK: Yeah, well,
there are no bugs in Go.

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:32.175
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Of course.

00:23:32.175 --> 00:23:34.610
QUINN SLACK: Except
when I write it.

00:23:34.610 --> 00:23:36.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So now I'm
curious about open source.

00:23:36.960 --> 00:23:39.950
You mentioned that
you're running--

00:23:39.950 --> 00:23:43.050
most of the thing is written
in Go, which is open source.

00:23:43.050 --> 00:23:45.310
Runs in Kubernetes,
which is open source.

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:47.080
You used to use gRPC--

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:50.550
not anymore, but gRPC
has also open source.

00:23:50.550 --> 00:23:52.050
What about other projects?

00:23:52.050 --> 00:23:54.472
Have you considered things
like Prometheus, Istio?

00:23:54.472 --> 00:23:55.680
What do you think about them?

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:57.500
QUINN SLACK: Yeah,
we do use Prometheus.

00:23:57.500 --> 00:24:02.640
We use Graphite, and we use
a lot of open-source language

00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:03.220
servers.

00:24:03.220 --> 00:24:05.910
We have released a lot of
open-source language servers

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:07.590
for language analysis.

00:24:07.590 --> 00:24:11.550
And we have a bunch of other
open-source projects that are

00:24:11.550 --> 00:24:15.060
on GitHub.com/Sourcegraph
that various people are using,

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:15.757
mostly in Go.

00:24:15.757 --> 00:24:17.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Cool, and all of this

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:20.310
is in Sourcegraph
on GitHub directly?

00:24:20.310 --> 00:24:22.012
GitHub.com/Sourcegraph?

00:24:22.012 --> 00:24:22.970
QUINN SLACK: That's it.

00:24:22.970 --> 00:24:24.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Easy, OK, cool.

00:24:24.450 --> 00:24:25.350
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:26.730
This all sounds super cool.

00:24:26.730 --> 00:24:28.140
I'll ask one question
as well, because now I'm

00:24:28.140 --> 00:24:30.640
just interested in Sourcegraph,
and I've never really played

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:31.180
with it.

00:24:31.180 --> 00:24:33.810
If I want to play with
Sourcegraph, where do I start?

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:36.330
Can I just use it on
GitHub for free, on that?

00:24:36.330 --> 00:24:37.920
Or do I have to pay?

00:24:37.920 --> 00:24:41.225
How do I play with this thing
if I'm quite keen on it?

00:24:41.225 --> 00:24:42.600
QUINN SLACK: Yeah,
Sourcegraph is

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:47.571
totally free for open source,
so just Go and Sourcegraph.com.

00:24:47.571 --> 00:24:50.070
You can sign up, and then you
can just start clicking around

00:24:50.070 --> 00:24:51.060
on your repositories.

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:54.510
Or just go to the Chrome Web
Store, type in Sourcegraph,

00:24:54.510 --> 00:24:56.520
get our Chrome extension,
and then just start

00:24:56.520 --> 00:24:58.290
looking at code on GitHub.

00:24:58.290 --> 00:24:59.790
If you want to look
at private code,

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:01.900
then you'll obviously
have to log in.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:04.070
But for open-source code,
it's just going to work.

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:06.631
Just put your mouse over a
token and you'll see the hover.

00:25:06.631 --> 00:25:08.630
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I cannot
recommend that enough.

00:25:08.630 --> 00:25:11.980
Just adding the
Chrome extension makes

00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:13.970
GitHub so much easier to use.

00:25:13.970 --> 00:25:17.060
Things like Go To
Definition on a web page--

00:25:17.060 --> 00:25:18.110
it just blows my mind.

00:25:18.110 --> 00:25:18.929
So useful.

00:25:18.929 --> 00:25:20.720
QUINN SLACK: Yeah, we
think that developers

00:25:20.720 --> 00:25:24.896
deserve things like Go To
Definition, Find References--

00:25:24.896 --> 00:25:26.270
all these things
anywhere they're

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:29.720
looking at code, whether it's
in their browser, their editor,

00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:31.270
random other web pages.

00:25:31.270 --> 00:25:32.270
It should be like water.

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:33.520
It should be always available.

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:34.436
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:25:34.436 --> 00:25:36.680
I'm going to ask one more
question that might be

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:38.090
a complicated question to ask.

00:25:38.090 --> 00:25:41.810
But we just did an
episode on Kubernetes 1.7,

00:25:41.810 --> 00:25:43.820
and there was a lot
of new features.

00:25:43.820 --> 00:25:45.920
Do you know if there's
any of these features that

00:25:45.920 --> 00:25:47.060
are interesting for you?

00:25:47.060 --> 00:25:51.230
Or are you more like a
core usage of Kubernetes,

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:52.820
like services and stuff?

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:54.919
QUINN SLACK: I don't know.

00:25:54.919 --> 00:25:56.460
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's totally fine.

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:57.335
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:25:57.335 --> 00:25:58.980
All right, well,
before we wrap up,

00:25:58.980 --> 00:26:01.501
is there anything that we
haven't managed to mention,

00:26:01.501 --> 00:26:02.750
or something you want to plug?

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.349
Maybe events you'll be at,
or particularly cool things

00:26:05.349 --> 00:26:06.890
you've seen from
Sourcegraph that you

00:26:06.890 --> 00:26:09.450
think will be on the
podcast before we finish up?

00:26:09.450 --> 00:26:12.770
QUINN SLACK: Well, I want to
just plug GopherCon, which

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:14.970
we went to a few months ago.

00:26:14.970 --> 00:26:16.490
We had a fantastic time.

00:26:16.490 --> 00:26:18.480
We had a bunch of
Sourcegraphers there,

00:26:18.480 --> 00:26:20.679
met a lot of our
users and customers.

00:26:20.679 --> 00:26:22.220
We did something
that was really fun,

00:26:22.220 --> 00:26:24.472
and I would encourage
everyone listening

00:26:24.472 --> 00:26:25.680
to participate in the future.

00:26:25.680 --> 00:26:27.410
We live blogged the conference.

00:26:27.410 --> 00:26:30.770
So we would just
sit in the talk,

00:26:30.770 --> 00:26:33.950
and we'd be typing as
the person was talking.

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:36.050
And at the end, we'd
have a live blog post

00:26:36.050 --> 00:26:37.580
that we could just
click Publish on.

00:26:37.580 --> 00:26:39.230
And then everyone who couldn't
make it at the conference

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:41.570
could see what was going
on and follow along.

00:26:41.570 --> 00:26:42.500
It was a ton of fun.

00:26:42.500 --> 00:26:44.690
We got a lot of community
volunteers to do it.

00:26:44.690 --> 00:26:47.870
And we're going be doing that
at a lot more conferences.

00:26:47.870 --> 00:26:50.570
Dot Go will be one of
them, and other conferences

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:51.870
in other languages as well.

00:26:51.870 --> 00:26:54.284
So if you hear of a
live blog going on,

00:26:54.284 --> 00:26:55.700
then shoot us an
email if you want

00:26:55.700 --> 00:26:58.292
to participate by writing
or even helping us edit.

00:26:58.292 --> 00:26:59.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:02.691
So thank you so much for being
so active in the Go community

00:27:02.691 --> 00:27:03.940
and the open-source community.

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:05.939
But also, thank you so
much for being here today

00:27:05.939 --> 00:27:08.850
and telling us a little
bit more about Sourcegraph

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:10.340
and what you do
with Google Cloud.

00:27:10.340 --> 00:27:10.890
QUINN SLACK: Thank you.

00:27:10.890 --> 00:27:12.590
Thank you for making it
so I can come and spend

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:14.256
some time with you
all instead of having

00:27:14.256 --> 00:27:17.066
to deal with all the
ops and systems work.

00:27:17.066 --> 00:27:18.440
You take care of
all that for us.

00:27:18.440 --> 00:27:19.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Awesome, thank you.

00:27:19.340 --> 00:27:20.923
MARK MANDEL: So a
huge thanks to Quinn

00:27:20.923 --> 00:27:24.440
for joining us on the podcast
to talk all about Sourcegraph.

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:26.870
Sounds like a super
interesting project.

00:27:26.870 --> 00:27:28.919
I'm actually-- I'm a
huge IntelliJ user.

00:27:28.919 --> 00:27:31.460
They've got integrations with
everything, as we talked about,

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:34.660
so I'm keen to get it installed
and see what interesting stuff

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:37.160
it can find out about my source,
and give me some nice hints

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:38.340
on what I can do with it.

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:39.256
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:39.256 --> 00:27:42.860
For me, really, it is just
the integration with Chrome

00:27:42.860 --> 00:27:46.070
that allows me to just
browse code on GitHub

00:27:46.070 --> 00:27:48.770
in a much easier way,
to the point that when

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:51.410
I use another profile
where it is not installed,

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:53.420
it just feels like
something is missing.

00:27:53.420 --> 00:27:56.090
So that's a good thing
for an extension.

00:27:56.090 --> 00:27:59.190
Anyway, it's time to go with
our question of the week.

00:27:59.190 --> 00:28:02.030
And the question of the
week is basically, OK,

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:05.900
so I have information, and
I'm storing information,

00:28:05.900 --> 00:28:09.080
but some of that information
should not be stored.

00:28:09.080 --> 00:28:12.680
Let's say that you have
a chat, for instance,

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:15.680
and normally, you expect
people to talk to you

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:16.977
and tell you things.

00:28:16.977 --> 00:28:18.560
And one of the things
that you may ask

00:28:18.560 --> 00:28:21.500
is, oh, just to make sure
that you're the right person,

00:28:21.500 --> 00:28:24.740
please give me the last four
digits of your credit card

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:26.420
or your social security number.

00:28:26.420 --> 00:28:27.830
The last four digits.

00:28:27.830 --> 00:28:30.110
And someone, by mistake,
gives the whole thing.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:30.860
MARK MANDEL: Oops.

00:28:30.860 --> 00:28:32.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
do you do then, right?

00:28:32.920 --> 00:28:34.160
You cannot just store that.

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:35.326
MARK MANDEL: No, that's bad.

00:28:35.326 --> 00:28:37.970
Or somebody sends a photo
of their license, maybe.

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:38.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:28:38.900 --> 00:28:41.300
There's a lot of
information that is private.

00:28:41.300 --> 00:28:42.980
Or even more than that.

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:45.770
You can have things like IPs.

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:48.832
Or a lot of information
that goes into logs,

00:28:48.832 --> 00:28:51.290
you just really want to make
sure that those things are not

00:28:51.290 --> 00:28:53.800
actually stored
and made persistent

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:56.420
so other people might
find them later on.

00:28:56.420 --> 00:28:58.867
Also, it goes against some--
a bunch of rules, actually.

00:28:58.867 --> 00:29:01.200
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, there's a
bunch of compliance stuff--

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:02.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, a
bunch of compliance things

00:29:02.540 --> 00:29:03.150
that [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:03.150 --> 00:29:04.566
MARK MANDEL: All
right, so I don't

00:29:04.566 --> 00:29:06.400
want to set up a huge
number of humans going

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.566
through all this stuff,
because that's obviously not

00:29:08.566 --> 00:29:09.330
going to scale.

00:29:09.330 --> 00:29:09.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So--

00:29:09.740 --> 00:29:11.823
MARK MANDEL: What tools
do I have available to me?

00:29:11.823 --> 00:29:13.157
FRANCESC CAMPOY: TensorFlow.

00:29:13.157 --> 00:29:14.740
MARK MANDEL: First,
we need the model.

00:29:14.740 --> 00:29:15.890
I think that's the
answer to everything.

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:17.431
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
You train the model.

00:29:17.431 --> 00:29:19.450
Actually, you could
technically do it, right?

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:22.070
With TensorFlow, you train it
so it recognizes what a credit

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:26.420
card is, then recognizes what
a Social Security card looks

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:27.900
like, and start with that.

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:30.157
And then you would be able
to delete those things.

00:29:30.157 --> 00:29:31.490
And that's what Google has done.

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:35.090
And rather than publishing
as a TensorFlow model,

00:29:35.090 --> 00:29:36.290
it is simply an API.

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:40.295
So it's kind of like Cloud
Vision API, where you just send

00:29:40.295 --> 00:29:42.410
an image and it classifies it.

00:29:42.410 --> 00:29:46.460
In this case, what you do is you
can send either text or images.

00:29:46.460 --> 00:29:49.750
And in the images, it
will find things inside

00:29:49.750 --> 00:29:53.510
of the image and sense
or redact the part

00:29:53.510 --> 00:29:58.290
that is actually PII, Personally
Identifiable Information.

00:29:58.290 --> 00:30:00.400
When you're using it,
you can say exactly what

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:01.740
parts you want to redact.

00:30:01.740 --> 00:30:03.560
So for instance,
you could say, I

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.700
do not consider faces to be
something that I should redact.

00:30:06.700 --> 00:30:08.890
Or you could say,
actually, yes, if there's

00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:13.510
a picture of anything, make sure
that there's no faces on it.

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:16.360
And there's things, for
instance, Google Street View.

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:18.550
You do not see faces
on Google Street View.

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:20.130
You could do the
same with this API.

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:21.100
It's very simple.

00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:22.480
The cool thing is
that I actually

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:25.750
demoed this in Sydney
last week on Wednesday,

00:30:25.750 --> 00:30:27.620
and it was lots of fun.

00:30:27.620 --> 00:30:31.190
So we'll have a link on the show
notes so you can check it out.

00:30:31.190 --> 00:30:33.130
It's a five-minute
demo, real quick.

00:30:33.130 --> 00:30:36.190
But it ends up with a pretty
good joke, in my opinion.

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:37.930
I made it myself, so--

00:30:37.930 --> 00:30:39.720
MARK MANDEL: So you're
a little biased.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:40.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
I'm a little bit biased,

00:30:40.870 --> 00:30:42.460
but I'd say it was
a pretty good joke.

00:30:42.460 --> 00:30:43.252
People laughed, so.

00:30:43.252 --> 00:30:44.293
But they were Australian.

00:30:44.293 --> 00:30:45.460
Maybe they were just nice.

00:30:45.460 --> 00:30:46.501
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, maybe.

00:30:46.501 --> 00:30:47.230
Maybe, maybe not.

00:30:47.230 --> 00:30:48.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: No, Australian
people are not that nice.

00:30:48.650 --> 00:30:49.890
No, just kidding.

00:30:49.890 --> 00:30:50.870
MARK MANDEL: Wonderful.

00:30:50.870 --> 00:30:52.930
All right, well, that sounds
like a great way of doing it.

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:54.471
And I've got a very
simple API that I

00:30:54.471 --> 00:30:57.115
don't have to learn TensorFlow,
or do [INAUDIBLE] transforms,

00:30:57.115 --> 00:30:58.240
or anything else like that?

00:30:58.240 --> 00:30:59.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Everything
you need to learn is JSON.

00:30:59.520 --> 00:31:00.686
MARK MANDEL: It's just JSON?

00:31:00.686 --> 00:31:01.731
That sounds fantastic.

00:31:01.731 --> 00:31:02.230
Awesome.

